libFuzzer harnesses for gnustep-base
====================================

These harnesses feed arbitrary bytes to the parsers and decoders that
commonly handle untrusted data:

  fuzz_plist       NSPropertyListSerialization, all formats (autodetected)
  fuzz_bplist      binary property lists only (input prefixed by "bplist00")
  fuzz_json        NSJSONSerialization
  fuzz_xml         GSSloppyXMLParser (GNUstep's own XML parser, also used
                   for XML property lists)
  fuzz_mime        GSMimeParser, whole document and in small chunks
  fuzz_keyed       NSKeyedUnarchiver
  fuzz_unarchiver  NSUnarchiver
  fuzz_portcoder   NSPortCoder (an incoming Distributed Objects message)

An NSException raised for bad input is expected behaviour and is caught;
crashes, sanitizer reports, hangs and excessive memory use are bugs.

Requirements: clang with libFuzzer and AddressSanitizer, and a
gnustep-base built (in this source tree) with instrumentation:

  ./configure
  make -C Source debug=yes \
    ADDITIONAL_OBJCFLAGS="-fsanitize=address,fuzzer-no-link -fno-omit-frame-pointer" \
    ADDITIONAL_CFLAGS="-fsanitize=address,fuzzer-no-link" \
    ADDITIONAL_LDFLAGS="-fsanitize=address"

Then:

  CC=clang Tests/fuzz/build.sh          # builds Tests/fuzz/bin/fuzz_*
  Tests/fuzz/bin/seedgen Tests/fuzz/corpus   # initial corpora
  Tests/fuzz/run.sh fuzz_plist 600      # fuzz for 10 minutes
  Tests/fuzz/triage.sh fuzz_plist       # one line per crash input

seedgen is built by build.sh along with the harnesses.  run.sh keeps
going after a crash (libFuzzer -fork=1 -ignore_crashes=1) and stores
reproducers in Tests/fuzz/crashes/<harness>/.  To reproduce one:

  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 Tests/fuzz/bin/fuzz_plist <file>

Leak detection is disabled because the runtime and some caches keep
objects alive for the life of the process.
